[{:db/id "q-36624/selection/679eeea12d5b084e58a99f88ace3809d12f8270378b868fe8259661a04b53cb9" :answer/id #uuid "f9cf2fdb-8911-5c9c-8386-1a7e0f4b9448" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36624/selection/c2ffc81e9090b00e6bc385967c01d92e4480a98e57495cc1981bb14c1c2d8a74" :answer/id #uuid "190a0123-d76a-5322-b949-50f62f5b1bd8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}<0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36624/selection/60913319a51205d71ddee0d90805b19aef46b9acf6ec75259abef17b78460605" :answer/id #uuid "9fb23d72-7994-5773-8074-96d457a7cb94" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{x}$ exists everywhere on the $xy$-plane" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36624/selection/9f4befc5ae2b9bbdc56dbd609d6059907a4ea061ebf8420d8a3ffc9f99d080da" :answer/id #uuid "8342c367-3b88-5f2d-bcf8-9a3ec3e2ef4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36624/selection/32d2131c6d3d469be9ef8fc1c490d0797aee63ccca208d4b726dfe3e60f8472d" :answer/id #uuid "1b74d970-ab89-56a9-830a-d6b9a85b29ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\le 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36624/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d27fc016-a43f-5f71-834d-134bce23c1ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-36624/selection/9f4befc5ae2b9bbdc56dbd609d6059907a4ea061ebf8420d8a3ffc9f99d080da" :answer-field/choices ["q-36624/selection/679eeea12d5b084e58a99f88ace3809d12f8270378b868fe8259661a04b53cb9" "q-36624/selection/c2ffc81e9090b00e6bc385967c01d92e4480a98e57495cc1981bb14c1c2d8a74" "q-36624/selection/60913319a51205d71ddee0d90805b19aef46b9acf6ec75259abef17b78460605" "q-36624/selection/9f4befc5ae2b9bbdc56dbd609d6059907a4ea061ebf8420d8a3ffc9f99d080da" "q-36624/selection/32d2131c6d3d469be9ef8fc1c490d0797aee63ccca208d4b726dfe3e60f8472d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-36624" :question/id #uuid "18ddac77-0ded-55cf-96b4-1d32b4183800" :question/math-academy-id "q-36624" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}),$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2} & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2} & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-36624/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8702032e-0795-5b51-9cc9-f89b35b64fa0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-36624"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-72003/selection/35b08cd4249c8d5cd5454f67a74a1d4558dc0320e8e5c67b5a6eeb615cba972f" :answer/id #uuid "3148eab3-0b75-5775-b738-357a4c594f3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ne z}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72003/selection/f0b2f7be29a3e70c9280ce53ad5a215a3bdc6b6fcc744acc40ebc11ae339486b" :answer/id #uuid "92514a59-50a3-5ba2-86e9-f65555809f1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy<z}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72003/selection/fc271bbc5ecb71aa27b1a077ed4cd7e1de1a2f4c069993dfbaaecc058c8c1a48" :answer/id #uuid "77ab28e6-bd9c-5b47-a808-34967eb13c9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>z}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72003/selection/3ea993830c48a34bd0cce452f95e573466449ca7eb3796e1045e1ca08d86bb4d" :answer/id #uuid "4015b873-b9f8-5e03-a4d1-e7d7ae607e9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge z}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72003/selection/e6209aca2eed6b7845fcea865cb81bcaf2414d1260ae389fc6f9f538c4b8152d" :answer/id #uuid "7cf4b8fa-dce1-573a-bec6-8289db3723ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\le z}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72003/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3c09a2-0ac1-5598-9123-353925213644" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-72003/selection/fc271bbc5ecb71aa27b1a077ed4cd7e1de1a2f4c069993dfbaaecc058c8c1a48" :answer-field/choices ["q-72003/selection/35b08cd4249c8d5cd5454f67a74a1d4558dc0320e8e5c67b5a6eeb615cba972f" "q-72003/selection/f0b2f7be29a3e70c9280ce53ad5a215a3bdc6b6fcc744acc40ebc11ae339486b" "q-72003/selection/fc271bbc5ecb71aa27b1a077ed4cd7e1de1a2f4c069993dfbaaecc058c8c1a48" "q-72003/selection/3ea993830c48a34bd0cce452f95e573466449ca7eb3796e1045e1ca08d86bb4d" "q-72003/selection/e6209aca2eed6b7845fcea865cb81bcaf2414d1260ae389fc6f9f538c4b8152d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-72003" :question/id #uuid "1b87df3e-a6bc-5a1a-8ef7-2548dce97717" :question/math-academy-id "q-72003" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{xy-z},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy-z & \\ge 0 \\\\ xy & \\ge z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge z}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy-z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xy-z}}\\cdot x \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy-z}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy-z & >0 \\\\ xy & >z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>z}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge z}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>z} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-72003/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8702032e-0795-5b51-9cc9-f89b35b64fa0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-72003"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132645/selection/7a139cc2dc87bac57c568f25b9971c0e8a7563e57982a82e1acbf9743749daae" :answer/id #uuid "fe1f3b1f-aa2d-5fbc-80dd-0a20104eded1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132645/selection/a634fb260d3406bbc3e90b52f0b1c0970959dfad1c6d19e122f2f54976fc95f3" :answer/id #uuid "16ca7188-21df-5171-ba37-e3b6a73e90c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132645/selection/299839f05f4859046992f66df58b84675b5606cd5012eb37fb48a814ca7e16c0" :answer/id #uuid "cbb93424-5074-50a9-abcb-e67974095a44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z\\ne 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132645/selection/856f5e63ab8ced1912ba00f97e40ab58e34991eb247fbc5fbb2ba72d93fb2b76" :answer/id #uuid "df99b2ec-312b-5d55-bcd5-f93f3eaffe36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y>0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132645/selection/82bf3b190819b2b437228dac1a5f8ee4a48b922803226f9c1afd999411bcc7ef" :answer/id #uuid "a42f3225-0102-58da-b3bf-2e1ad5e4bb14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y>0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132645/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e6891fc5-4b5c-5983-8d8a-1601638739d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-132645/selection/7a139cc2dc87bac57c568f25b9971c0e8a7563e57982a82e1acbf9743749daae" :answer-field/choices ["q-132645/selection/7a139cc2dc87bac57c568f25b9971c0e8a7563e57982a82e1acbf9743749daae" "q-132645/selection/a634fb260d3406bbc3e90b52f0b1c0970959dfad1c6d19e122f2f54976fc95f3" "q-132645/selection/299839f05f4859046992f66df58b84675b5606cd5012eb37fb48a814ca7e16c0" "q-132645/selection/856f5e63ab8ced1912ba00f97e40ab58e34991eb247fbc5fbb2ba72d93fb2b76" "q-132645/selection/82bf3b190819b2b437228dac1a5f8ee4a48b922803226f9c1afd999411bcc7ef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132645" :question/id #uuid "27098d48-7989-5b6b-ace6-3cb96c67f741" :question/math-academy-id "q-132645" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)}{y},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+z & >0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+z} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y(x+z)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{z}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+z & \\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $z$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-132645/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8702032e-0795-5b51-9cc9-f89b35b64fa0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132645"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-72000/selection/129cfd8e646a841031e392e103b7ee15276f6aaf86c08c2d77097ea3093eff6f" :answer/id #uuid "98e1d76a-95fd-5cff-89a7-935c66a0a7dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z=0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72000/selection/cfd08a86e6662aa902bc602caea0761421279fe82c35c90e1cee722817a8723d" :answer/id #uuid "38ee16b9-f4f9-5eab-8030-3fbc11de5e63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ge 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72000/selection/d32e7a5918dbbe197631c8b3bc15745170e8d8e5a2c66be7876df5da8a55d0aa" :answer/id #uuid "6b2aa66b-1dd8-5e48-b39c-3309746ca2bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72000/selection/2b9a311863cd85eba563cead7bb8db53fd67f3b167af6bc89f206cae77d935ec" :answer/id #uuid "60e3fcd2-0b47-5157-a6fa-9b0311a2bfa6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\le 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72000/selection/2c1f4e4085dedb86cf5cbea71b5fc57d13832015ab4b8f529067923b46318c0f" :answer/id #uuid "c6a8ec84-87b8-5206-a9d4-34183a879471" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72000/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ccf78f1b-49bd-59f9-8276-4dac4e64921a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-72000/selection/2c1f4e4085dedb86cf5cbea71b5fc57d13832015ab4b8f529067923b46318c0f" :answer-field/choices ["q-72000/selection/129cfd8e646a841031e392e103b7ee15276f6aaf86c08c2d77097ea3093eff6f" "q-72000/selection/cfd08a86e6662aa902bc602caea0761421279fe82c35c90e1cee722817a8723d" "q-72000/selection/d32e7a5918dbbe197631c8b3bc15745170e8d8e5a2c66be7876df5da8a55d0aa" "q-72000/selection/2b9a311863cd85eba563cead7bb8db53fd67f3b167af6bc89f206cae77d935ec" "q-72000/selection/2c1f4e4085dedb86cf5cbea71b5fc57d13832015ab4b8f529067923b46318c0f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-72000" :question/id #uuid "bc1eed40-8b7c-53f0-933f-85fd9db90170" :question/math-academy-id "q-72000" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z),$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{z}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $z$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-72000/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8702032e-0795-5b51-9cc9-f89b35b64fa0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-72000"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132643/selection/b3e0b6343af0054e544bfe9df2c7a4a0952112fada875d3b97b2f5e1651e0900" :answer/id #uuid "0f6f8f70-7d2f-5b5c-b14d-7803ceb15330" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-z>0\\,\\text{and}\\,z\\ne 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132643/selection/16ca280363db83e5f01c55cb3daf88031dfdbe1af5f75d268fd44e034f391a45" :answer/id #uuid "d7db425f-92aa-5505-8595-79eaffae9552" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y\\ne 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132643/selection/9b3bbd9969da2418fc3cfe9092ffa8e219253045e6ead6254cf1f7f997459027" :answer/id #uuid "a0223643-4d06-5c12-a9ce-b08f40b8da8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132643/selection/10d5b2fd211f294d8a22fb6fe03c82819f56b11ef701ff371ff890480f51dbae" :answer/id #uuid "cb29efab-50a2-577e-bf75-d97d809f475c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-z\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,z>0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132643/selection/004e2ee05d1be60661f8d64c8a0c4bf4c8e928579e659cd31c846aa4fd74a256" :answer/id #uuid "4f807010-9735-5b5f-868d-a3eadaec52de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132643/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c4044f33-0f97-50e9-8796-35b8aa64c4c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-132643/selection/9b3bbd9969da2418fc3cfe9092ffa8e219253045e6ead6254cf1f7f997459027" :answer-field/choices ["q-132643/selection/b3e0b6343af0054e544bfe9df2c7a4a0952112fada875d3b97b2f5e1651e0900" "q-132643/selection/16ca280363db83e5f01c55cb3daf88031dfdbe1af5f75d268fd44e034f391a45" "q-132643/selection/9b3bbd9969da2418fc3cfe9092ffa8e219253045e6ead6254cf1f7f997459027" "q-132643/selection/10d5b2fd211f294d8a22fb6fe03c82819f56b11ef701ff371ff890480f51dbae" "q-132643/selection/004e2ee05d1be60661f8d64c8a0c4bf4c8e928579e659cd31c846aa4fd74a256"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132643" :question/id #uuid "20a0786c-29f2-5827-9394-c6b48bd4e191" :question/math-academy-id "q-132643" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{x-y}},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{x-y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{(x-y)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-132643/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8702032e-0795-5b51-9cc9-f89b35b64fa0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132643"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-72005/selection/1c8e64bf6af7ca3407ea17f2911fc85e00b7e78c79165760b0fdf77697161ccc" :answer/id #uuid "4a5e107b-3f50-5bd8-bbcd-305062ce3cdf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72005/selection/21816421f3d8ff6389df8f8fe97c708eb808b778c157ab56c9079c8a67aa7e9f" :answer/id #uuid "d6f67e50-fb3e-5c99-b805-8da7432be84a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72005/selection/b9f72e3c267a49a4e030fb519af8c7c463c96c3997885cba6009eee2b235f129" :answer/id #uuid "42764dc4-d6a8-54de-ab9c-6e12289fb360" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72005/selection/57b28e924f8802f53ac6262cee0ca3f632efeaa805d7bb75d7272d329622ac70" :answer/id #uuid "41a4562c-39f9-59ee-9f23-b100e444c6fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72005/selection/5924959ce7dc25c9af0789cbb6117d3d456cd029bc51f51d982755d42fdce11b" :answer/id #uuid "f2815d82-add3-5b62-b7a6-1f96bccbae5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72005/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea54a922-cd8d-5604-8b3d-e3c059805f1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-72005/selection/5924959ce7dc25c9af0789cbb6117d3d456cd029bc51f51d982755d42fdce11b" :answer-field/choices ["q-72005/selection/1c8e64bf6af7ca3407ea17f2911fc85e00b7e78c79165760b0fdf77697161ccc" "q-72005/selection/21816421f3d8ff6389df8f8fe97c708eb808b778c157ab56c9079c8a67aa7e9f" "q-72005/selection/b9f72e3c267a49a4e030fb519af8c7c463c96c3997885cba6009eee2b235f129" "q-72005/selection/57b28e924f8802f53ac6262cee0ca3f632efeaa805d7bb75d7272d329622ac70" "q-72005/selection/5924959ce7dc25c9af0789cbb6117d3d456cd029bc51f51d982755d42fdce11b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-72005" :question/id #uuid "ed7bcfe5-c1ea-56d9-b1e4-351fef6e9306" :question/math-academy-id "q-72005" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-72005/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-72005"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132651/selection/10d68f15f089c58365613a02e8180a8c45ac6144620ecdfce4995f040d8a3ed9" :answer/id #uuid "2ef044d8-00e4-5a86-97b5-193928d27dc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ne 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132651/selection/627bc16eb8267a1beb4e45621c68b7a9c1ecad38eb79f4728502b5077ded8c86" :answer/id #uuid "31da173d-c83c-5760-8198-7a58e9e2bd59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y<0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132651/selection/ad06cb172d17ae978ad0f12d642a2bf0c986d7336333cf41a5779c77c102a6eb" :answer/id #uuid "e1f01b9a-228d-5e66-ac6a-646a3cef7a1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x<0,\\,y\\ne 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132651/selection/58523aaa8cb56a3b6297e73f4ff7236bcf24511349ce9f939f8d819d407b5569" :answer/id #uuid "58bcff5a-e634-500e-bc89-63a21964363f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y>0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132651/selection/b02cad936aba824aff2529ed380683d534bdfdf1b5b54fedc94e5a36bcbda099" :answer/id #uuid "c3df19f4-b5e0-5c0e-a8fa-e953ef42c0d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132651/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1aeb9b2f-c57d-585e-8b91-7b853b9a6e87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-132651/selection/b02cad936aba824aff2529ed380683d534bdfdf1b5b54fedc94e5a36bcbda099" :answer-field/choices ["q-132651/selection/10d68f15f089c58365613a02e8180a8c45ac6144620ecdfce4995f040d8a3ed9" "q-132651/selection/627bc16eb8267a1beb4e45621c68b7a9c1ecad38eb79f4728502b5077ded8c86" "q-132651/selection/ad06cb172d17ae978ad0f12d642a2bf0c986d7336333cf41a5779c77c102a6eb" "q-132651/selection/58523aaa8cb56a3b6297e73f4ff7236bcf24511349ce9f939f8d819d407b5569" "q-132651/selection/b02cad936aba824aff2529ed380683d534bdfdf1b5b54fedc94e5a36bcbda099"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132651" :question/id #uuid "ebb24a0e-97b7-5573-87dd-3d7664af2b3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-132651" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{y}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{2y\\sqrt{x}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{y}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2y\\sqrt{x}}$ is defined only when $x>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-132651/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132651"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132650/selection/213d7e1ba17d1e425cbb585869fdbfc8d14f291cfb45221b445655377e4ba968" :answer/id #uuid "ebd16056-188b-5412-b1c9-b33a5b3b32ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132650/selection/b7758dfb7d279b8cffe7da65ad8052d28a9edb053bb6b839e341f29f02098c33" :answer/id #uuid "d0fa6ca5-8fd5-5f3c-be48-4fe1ad398997" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132650/selection/c6ef95b8172bca4c20a43a337e02d81381180274c3f99f0e828123554b2173df" :answer/id #uuid "845b9aa7-8ebe-5fab-b45c-1503b466a436" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0,\\,z>0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132650/selection/49873be477ec1bb3547b033f012f963ec9f51c84ffb07c417ec7285bc3bc5f76" :answer/id #uuid "7702e375-c362-5b2a-915f-b22332338a2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132650/selection/6561f36ec485710f2b0a6147ec6f58860e76b77e5297e7aa907a1ad0aa4eba83" :answer/id #uuid "0fa92c70-ae91-5c73-b1cd-b9c1b0396a39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132650/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ca829a24-7567-5ce3-8954-df7154fedce3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-132650/selection/213d7e1ba17d1e425cbb585869fdbfc8d14f291cfb45221b445655377e4ba968" :answer-field/choices ["q-132650/selection/213d7e1ba17d1e425cbb585869fdbfc8d14f291cfb45221b445655377e4ba968" "q-132650/selection/b7758dfb7d279b8cffe7da65ad8052d28a9edb053bb6b839e341f29f02098c33" "q-132650/selection/c6ef95b8172bca4c20a43a337e02d81381180274c3f99f0e828123554b2173df" "q-132650/selection/49873be477ec1bb3547b033f012f963ec9f51c84ffb07c417ec7285bc3bc5f76" "q-132650/selection/6561f36ec485710f2b0a6147ec6f58860e76b77e5297e7aa907a1ad0aa4eba83"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132650" :question/id #uuid "1579cb82-f401-5fee-aadc-be6820a852e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-132650" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{z}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{2y}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂z}=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{z}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{2x}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1\\ne 0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${g}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is the same: \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{z}(x,y,z)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{{z}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{z}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{z}} & ={D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{z}} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-132650/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132650"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-72025/selection/16d5311a3b61341ffdec83edc66223ff42ba40a4ad6492ead64e9d44292e3657" :answer/id #uuid "b751beaf-5c3b-5a76-b629-23e9e15e36aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72025/selection/d32e7a5918dbbe197631c8b3bc15745170e8d8e5a2c66be7876df5da8a55d0aa" :answer/id #uuid "f04f6c78-3349-5dc8-9c4e-4b3d0bb39cf6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72025/selection/2c83551bb159610db1d722c14fd51213b79582b9caa474a7a6aea95058918d1e" :answer/id #uuid "86aeab84-486a-593d-8364-749137a8584d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x+y+z)^{2}>1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72025/selection/9e60b44e63ddef550d67a4bacac773aa1939044bf5b8119332c6b508db9fc164" :answer/id #uuid "aba573cd-cbb6-51e0-90b5-209daadee27e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72025/selection/91e055d9167f0b9c3945600e9ad407edb2f6e3f265b5a6c01a980d153b1aff90" :answer/id #uuid "0fbcc1cd-4f49-5497-aeec-b1d2b9510939" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72025/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d58194a7-e7d3-5ca0-ae88-f9905cc9f289" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-72025/selection/9e60b44e63ddef550d67a4bacac773aa1939044bf5b8119332c6b508db9fc164" :answer-field/choices ["q-72025/selection/16d5311a3b61341ffdec83edc66223ff42ba40a4ad6492ead64e9d44292e3657" "q-72025/selection/d32e7a5918dbbe197631c8b3bc15745170e8d8e5a2c66be7876df5da8a55d0aa" "q-72025/selection/2c83551bb159610db1d722c14fd51213b79582b9caa474a7a6aea95058918d1e" "q-72025/selection/9e60b44e63ddef550d67a4bacac773aa1939044bf5b8119332c6b508db9fc164" "q-72025/selection/91e055d9167f0b9c3945600e9ad407edb2f6e3f265b5a6c01a980d153b1aff90"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-72025" :question/id #uuid "735926a2-d3a9-5db6-a4eb-54bb660a7efe" :question/math-academy-id "q-72025" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y+z)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{1+(x+y+z)^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y+z)$ is defined for any $x$, $y$, and $z.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}=\\frac{1}{1+(x+y+z)^{2}}$ is defined for any $x$, $y$, and $z.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-72025/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-72025"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-72008/selection/53ca804ce60c64653ec9b765d43ffdd54f60b08f606b02d48b9773f59ddb0200" :answer/id #uuid "1d37609d-2ab7-5894-8b99-06eb1b65babd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y>0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72008/selection/520300d83254080f9f80224bc16540d01963cb98dea6ad5ec016c33e92cab645" :answer/id #uuid "f923fac3-f4d2-5047-b3f4-41b9e7fad34d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x=0,y\\ne 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72008/selection/fcd78aa19638b0af1e68373dde067689acb3936917c6f27a8665b96e32acf6ef" :answer/id #uuid "8d5e0a5e-1dec-5ee0-b6c9-f1535238339c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y>0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72008/selection/1f901647d6c6d98a2b550bd7507f5d174d1d844aeb951c7ff55a17ef8237cd69" :answer/id #uuid "ea5cff9a-aae1-5f98-9b2f-4632f52d422e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72008/selection/71a97c7199fa73b98da8f905c7e9c2aa79aa3e75a4aca92ba1cbf58c5db1d16b" :answer/id #uuid "8bee1b94-cbc2-5f5c-814a-7e785113db2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72008/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f30ecd9d-bb53-5315-933c-e7c026c37751" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-72008/selection/1f901647d6c6d98a2b550bd7507f5d174d1d844aeb951c7ff55a17ef8237cd69" :answer-field/choices ["q-72008/selection/53ca804ce60c64653ec9b765d43ffdd54f60b08f606b02d48b9773f59ddb0200" "q-72008/selection/520300d83254080f9f80224bc16540d01963cb98dea6ad5ec016c33e92cab645" "q-72008/selection/fcd78aa19638b0af1e68373dde067689acb3936917c6f27a8665b96e32acf6ef" "q-72008/selection/1f901647d6c6d98a2b550bd7507f5d174d1d844aeb951c7ff55a17ef8237cd69" "q-72008/selection/71a97c7199fa73b98da8f905c7e9c2aa79aa3e75a4aca92ba1cbf58c5db1d16b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-72008" :question/id #uuid "4837a60f-bb8d-504f-a02b-562568935e07" :question/math-academy-id "q-72008" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{(2x+y){e}^{x/y}}{2y\\sqrt{x}},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}\\,{e}^{x/y}}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{(2x+y){e}^{x/y}}{2y\\sqrt{x}}$ is defined only when $x>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}{e}^{x/y}}{{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-72008/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-72008"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-36504"] :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{x+y})?$\n\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,1)$\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $f$ is defined only when\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{x+y}>0.\n$$\n\nAt points in the domain of $f,$ we can compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1\\cdot (x+y)-x\\cdot 1}{(x+y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{y}{(x+y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x(x+y)} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{(x+y)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is defined at $(1,1),$ since\n\n$$\nf(1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{1+1})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}),\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are defined there:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =\\frac{1}{1(1+1)}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =-\\frac{1}{1+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. At $(0,1),$\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{x+y}=\\frac{0}{0+1}=0,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined. Therefore, ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is not defined.\n\n- Statement III is false for the same reason. Since $f$ is not defined at $(0,1),$ the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is not defined. The simplified expression $-\\frac{1}{x+y}$ cannot be used to define the partial derivative at a point outside the domain of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-36522"] :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}?$\n\n- The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(1,-1,0)$\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1,1)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{z}(1,1,0)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot (-2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(1,-1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,-1,0)=\\frac{(0)^{2}}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1,1)=\\frac{(1)^{2}}{(0)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=-1,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1,1) & =-\\frac{2(0)(1)}{(0)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(1,1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,1,0)=\\frac{(0)^{2}}{(1)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-72022/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "5ae34c86-060d-53ab-9ac1-4580b0daf349" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72022/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "06ca2fb4-4e7c-5a9a-a5c1-257e86e17552" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72022/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "9dc4fec1-ee85-539d-a9a6-020f636a53ad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72022/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "4edc3dcd-37ba-53d9-afb4-aedfb4e26a0b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72022/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "dd1c5969-6293-54fd-abea-f33d4be9d44a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72022/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc04a0bb-2e4a-592a-819b-1a1e5e900b37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-72022/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-72022/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-72022/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-72022/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-72022/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-72022/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-72022" :question/id #uuid "929098a3-e3fe-5fb1-aaac-1e50c43eff3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72022" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy(z-1))?$\n\n- The partial derivative with respect to $z$ is defined at $(3,2,1)$\n- ${f}_{y}(1,1,2)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{x}(1,2,3)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{xy(z-1)}\\cdot y(z-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{xy(z-1)}\\cdot x(z-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{xy(z-1)}\\cdot xy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{z-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.  \n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(3,2,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(3,2,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot 2\\cdot (1-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\n- Statement II is true. If we substitute $(1,1,2)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,1,2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot (2-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,1,2) & =\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we substitute $(1,2,3)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,2,3)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 2\\cdot (3-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4),\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(1,2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,2,3) & =\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III  only'." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-72022/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-72022"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132675/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "e22a56cc-af57-5688-9905-3a73885bc07e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132675/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "53429129-c47b-5edb-8721-1e4c9a35b804" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132675/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "50760f06-568a-554b-974b-998aa4496d42" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132675/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "0fa85b43-88d9-5ca4-af25-92ff251fb0e0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132675/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "fcec31f0-e6aa-5123-9ae9-298ae226d18c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132675/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0de7b80b-5f05-59c1-a618-c8892b6e34a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-132675/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-132675/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-132675/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-132675/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-132675/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-132675/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132675" :question/id #uuid "5934557b-111f-5bfc-a8d6-ea9621318b4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-132675" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)?$\n\n- The partial derivative with respect to $y$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1,1)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{z}(1,-1,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(z) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(-1,-1,0)=0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡((-1)\\cdot (-1))=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(-1,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,-1,0) & =\\frac{0}{(-1)}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1,1)=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,-1,1)$ into ${f}_{z}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{z}(1,-1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot (-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-132675/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132675"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132728/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "8f1671e8-25ed-5742-884f-083d45a15ed9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132728/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "97674ece-fd1a-5cf1-83c9-b385e72ed6ce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132728/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "3343b8b3-61fa-59dc-b89f-11053150ab75" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132728/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "d39d75d2-2db6-596b-8542-38cb5d084810" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132728/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "28263756-4556-55be-bba1-c952dc89cb87" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132728/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a7797787-569b-5b73-b30f-a9b874e681b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-132728/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-132728/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-132728/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-132728/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-132728/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-132728/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132728" :question/id #uuid "c8cb7726-770d-51b1-bf05-a04aa01afc5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-132728" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2)?$\n\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,2)$\n- ${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(1,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(2)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{1}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-132728/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132728"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3449"] :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac yx,$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the partial derivative of a function exists where the original function is defined *and* the partial derivative is defined. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x,y)$ is not defined when $x=0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\nD_f = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x\\ne 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ is also not defined for $x=0.$ So, the domain of $f_y$ is\n\n$$\nD_{f_y} = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x\\ne 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the set where  $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists, we take the intersection of the domain of the original function $(D_f)$ with the domain of the partial derivative $(D_{f_y})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists on the set $\\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, x \\neq 0 \\}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6109"] :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y^2}\\right)$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = -\\dfrac{2}{y},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the partial derivatives of a function exist where the original function is defined and the partial derivatives are defined.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x,y) = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y^2}\\right)$ is defined only when $\\dfrac{x}{y^2}>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\nD_f = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x > 0, \\: y \\neq 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following:\n\n- The partial derivative $f_x(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x \\neq 0.$ So, the domain of $f_x$ is \n\n$$\nD_{f_x} = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x \\neq 0 \\}.\n$$\n\n- The partial derivative $f_y(x,y) =  -\\dfrac{2}{y}$ is defined only when $y \\neq 0.$ So, the domain of $f_y$ is \n\n$$\nD_{f_y} = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, y \\neq 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3450"] :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x,y,z)=xz\\ln{(x+y+z)}?$\n\n- $f_x(-1,-1,-1)$ is defined\n\n- $f_y(1,0,-1)$ is defined\n\n- $f_z(1,1,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+\\frac{xz}{x+y+z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{xz}{x+y+z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+\\frac{xz}{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(-1,-1,-1)$ into $f,$ we get\n\n$$\nf(-1,-1,-1) = (-1)(-1)\\ln{(-1-1-1)} = \\ln{(-3)},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since the logarithm exists only for positive arguments. Therefore, $f_x$ cannot exist at this point.\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0,-1)$ into $f_y,$ we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0,-1) = \\dfrac{(1)(-1)}{1+0-1} = \\dfrac{-1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(1,1,1)= (1)(1)\\ln(1+1+1)=\\ln(3)\n$$\n\nLikewise $f_z$ is also defined at $(1,1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(1,1,1) & =(1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1+1)+\\frac{(1)(1)}{1+1+1}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
